use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

/*
    Tutorial: Combo Boxes, part of the user interface series.
*/
class UserInterfacesComboBox is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/ui/comboBox.html")
        SetShortTitle("Combo Boxes")
        SetSectionName("User Interfaces")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("How to add combo boxes to a Quorum form, fill them with choices, read the chosen choice, check typed text and react when the choice changes.")

        Title("Combo Boxes")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("A combo box is a text field with a list of choices attached to it. You meet
them when a website asks for your state, your shirt size or your country. A
person can open the list and pick a choice, or start typing, and the list
shrinks to the choices that match. In this tutorial, you will add combo boxes
to a form, fill them with choices, find out which choice was picked, and make
your program react the moment the choice changes.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Paragraph("You have the following goals for this tutorial:")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Add a combo box to a form with "):Code("AddComboBox"):Text(" and fill it with choices")
        ListItem("Read the chosen choice with "):Code("GetSelectedIndex"):Text(" and "):Code("GetSelectedItem")
        ListItem("Use a "):Code("ComboBoxListener"):Text(" to react when a person picks a different choice")
        ListItem("Check whether typed text matches one of the choices")
        ListItem("Explain how a person using a keyboard or a screen reader uses a combo box")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think of a form where you had to pick from a long list, such as a country or
a birth year. Did you scroll through the list, or did you type the first few
letters? What would make a long list of choices easier to use for someone
who can't see the screen, or who can't use a mouse?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/userInterface/ComboBox/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/userInterface/ComboBox/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        Paragraph("This tutorial covers the following "):Link("CSTA standards", "https://csteachers.org/pk12standards/view/"):Text(":")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_IMPACTS_OF_ALGORITHMS_AND_DESIGN_9)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_DATA_COLLECTION_AND_PREPARATION_24)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_DATA_COLLECTION_AND_PREPARATION_24)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        WhatIsAComboBox()
        MakingAComboBox()
        PickingAChoice()
        FindingTheChoice()
        CheckingTypedText()
        ReactingToChanges()
        WhatAScreenReaderSays()
        Colors()
    end

    private action WhatIsAComboBox
        Subsection("What is a combo box?")
        Paragraph("In the "):Link("Text Fields", "/tutorials/ui/textField.html"):Text(" tutorial, a person could
type anything into a field. Sometimes a program wants one answer from a
short list instead, such as a shirt size. A combo box does both jobs. It is a
text field, so a person can type in it, and it has a list of choices that
opens below the field.")

        Paragraph("In Quorum, the "):Code("ComboBox"):Text(" class comes from the "):Code("TextField"):Text(" class. That means
a combo box can do everything a text field can do, such as "):Code("GetText"):Text(", plus
keep a list of choices and remember which one is chosen.")

        Paragraph("A combo box isn't the only way to offer choices. "):Link("Radio buttons", "/tutorials/ui/radioButton.html"):Text(" show every choice at once, which is
good for two to five choices. A combo box keeps the choices hidden until the
list opens, so it fits a long list, such as a list of countries, in a small
space.")
    end

    private action MakingAComboBox
        Subsection("Making a combo box on a form")
        Paragraph("The easiest way to make a combo box is on a form, which you met in the "):Link("Forms", "/tutorials/ui/forms.html"):Text(" tutorial. A page's "):Code("AddComboBox"):Text(" action
makes a combo box, adds it to the page and returns it so that you can change
it. There are two versions:")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("AddComboBox(name, items)"):Text(" takes an "):Code("Array"):Text(" of text with the choices in
order;")
        ListItem():Code("AddComboBox(name)"):Text(" makes an empty combo box, and you add choices with its "):Code("Add"):Text(" action.")

        Paragraph("The name is what a screen reader says when the combo box gets the focus. A
label added with "):Code("AddLabel"):Text(" just before the combo box is shown beside it, the
same way a label sits beside a text field.")

        Paragraph("The program below makes a T-shirt order form with two combo boxes and a
button.")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/ComboBox/Main.quorum")

        Paragraph("The size box starts empty, so it shows its placeholder, "):DoubleQuote():Text("Choose a size."):DoubleQuote():Text(" The
color box starts with "):DoubleQuote():Text("Black"):DoubleQuote():Text(" chosen, because "):Code("Select(0)"):Text(" chose the choice at
index 0. Remember that indexes start at 0: "):DoubleQuote():Text("Black"):DoubleQuote():Text(" is 0, "):DoubleQuote():Text("Blue"):DoubleQuote():Text(" is 1, and so
on.")
    end

    private action PickingAChoice
        Subsection("Picking a choice")
        Paragraph("A person can pick a choice in several ways:")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("With the mouse:"):Text(" click the field to open the list, then click a choice.
Clicking the field again, or anywhere outside the list, closes it.")
        ListItem():Strong("With the arrow keys:"):Text(" press "):Key("Down"):Text(" to open the list, then "):Key("Down"):Text(" and "):Key("Up"):Text(" to move through it. The highlight moves, and the field's text stays the same
until you choose. Press "):Key("Enter"):Text(" to choose it.")
        ListItem():Strong("By typing:"):Text(" type the first letters of a choice. The list opens and
shows only the choices that start with what you typed, ignoring upper and
lower case. Typing "):DoubleQuote():Text("g"):DoubleQuote():Text(" in the color box leaves "):DoubleQuote():Text("Gray"):DoubleQuote():Text(" and "):DoubleQuote():Text("Green."):DoubleQuote()

        Paragraph("Here are all of a combo box's keys:")

        Table("A combo box's keys")
        TableHeader():Cell("Key"):Cell("When the list is closed"):Cell("When the list is open")
        TableRow():Cell("Typing"):Cell("Opens the list with only the matching choices"):Cell("Narrows the list")
        TableRowWriter downRow = TableRow()
        downRow:Cell():Key("Down"):Text(" or "):Keys("Alt", "Down")
        downRow:Cell("Opens the list")
        downRow:Cell("Moves to the next choice")
        TableRowWriter upRow = TableRow()
        upRow:Cell():Key("Up")
        upRow:Cell("Opens the list")
        upRow:Cell("Moves to the previous choice")
        TableRowWriter homeRow = TableRow()
        homeRow:Cell():Key("Home"):Text(", "):Key("End")
        homeRow:Cell("Moves to the start or end of the text")
        homeRow:Cell("Moves to the first or last choice")
        TableRowWriter enterRow = TableRow()
        enterRow:Cell():Key("Enter")
        enterRow:Cell("Does nothing in the combo box")
        enterRow:Cell("Chooses the highlighted choice and closes the list")
        TableRowWriter escapeRow = TableRow()
        escapeRow:Cell():Key("Escape")
        escapeRow:Cell("Right after the list closed, puts back the text the field had when it opened")
        escapeRow:Cell("Closes the list")
        TableRowWriter tabRow = TableRow()
        tabRow:Cell():Key("Tab")
        tabRow:Cell("Moves to the next control")
        tabRow:Cell("Closes the list, keeps the text, and moves on")

        Paragraph("When the focus leaves a combo box, it looks at the text in the field. If the
text is exactly one of the choices, that choice is chosen. If it isn't,
nothing is chosen. So typing "):DoubleQuote():Text("Medium"):DoubleQuote():Text(" and pressing "):Key("Tab"):Text(" chooses "):DoubleQuote():Text("Medium."):DoubleQuote()
    end

    private action FindingTheChoice
        Subsection("Finding out which choice was picked")
        Paragraph("A combo box has two actions for reading the choice:")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("GetSelectedIndex"):Text(" returns the chosen choice's index, or -1 if nothing is
chosen;")
        ListItem():Code("GetSelectedItem"):Text(" returns the chosen choice's text, or "):Code("undefined"):Text(" if
nothing is chosen.")

        Paragraph("The "):DoubleQuote():Text("Place order"):DoubleQuote():Text(" button uses both. Make a new class in a file named "):Code("OrderBehavior.quorum"):Text(". Like the behaviors in the "):Link("Checkboxes", "/tutorials/ui/checkbox.html"):Text(" tutorial, it comes from "):Code("FormBehavior"):Text(", which gives it the "):Code("GetPage"):Text(" action, so it can find each combo
box by name with "):Code("GetComboBox"):Text(".")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/ComboBox/OrderBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph("Run the program. Choose "):DoubleQuote():Text("Large"):DoubleQuote():Text(" and "):DoubleQuote():Text("Blue"):DoubleQuote():Text(" and press "):DoubleQuote():Text("Place order."):DoubleQuote():Text(" The
output says you ordered a Blue shirt, size Large. Now type "):DoubleQuote():Text("Huge"):DoubleQuote():Text(" in the size
box and press "):DoubleQuote():Text("Place order."):DoubleQuote():Text(" "):DoubleQuote():Text("Huge"):DoubleQuote():Text(" isn't one of the choices, so nothing is
chosen, and the program asks for a size from the list.")

        BlockEditorFile("OrderBehavior.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/ComboBox/OrderBehavior.quorum")
        BlockEditor("Main.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/ComboBox/Main.quorum", "", true, true)
    end

    private action CheckingTypedText
        Subsection("Checking typed text")
        Paragraph("Because a person can type anything into a combo box, the text in the field
isn't always one of the choices. That is a "):Strong("data quality"):Text(" problem: a
program that expects "):DoubleQuote():Text("Small,"):DoubleQuote():Text(" "):DoubleQuote():Text("Medium,"):DoubleQuote():Text(" "):DoubleQuote():Text("Large"):DoubleQuote():Text(" or "):DoubleQuote():Text("Extra large"):DoubleQuote():Text(" might get "):DoubleQuote():Text("Huge,"):DoubleQuote():Text(" "):DoubleQuote():Text("large"):DoubleQuote():Text(" or "):DoubleQuote():Text("L."):DoubleQuote():Text(" A combo box gives you two ways to tell the
difference:")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("GetText"):Text(", from "):Code("TextField"):Text(", returns whatever is in the field;")
        ListItem():Code("GetSelectedIndex"):Text(" returns -1 when that text isn't exactly one of the
choices.")

        Paragraph("The order behavior above chose one way to handle a bad answer: it refuses the
order and asks again. Other programs might take the closest choice, or keep
the new text as a new choice. Which one is best depends on what the answer is
used for.")
    end

    private action ReactingToChanges
        Subsection("Reacting when the choice changes")
        Paragraph("Sometimes a program should react the moment a person picks a choice, without
waiting for a button. A combo box tells its "):Strong("listeners"):Text(" each time a person
picks a different choice: by clicking it, by pressing "):Key("Enter"):Text(", or by leaving
the field with text that chooses a different one.")

        Paragraph("A listener is a class that comes from "):Code("ComboBoxListener"):Text(" and writes the "):Code("ComboBoxChanged"):Text(" action. The action gets a "):Code("ComboBoxChangedEvent"):Text(", which
has:")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("GetNewIndex"):Text(", the index of the new choice, or -1 for none;")
        ListItem():Code("GetOldIndex"):Text(", the index before the change;")
        ListItem():Code("GetText"):Text(", the new choice's text, or the field's text when nothing is
chosen;")
        ListItem():Code("GetComboBox"):Text(", the combo box that changed.")

        Paragraph("Make a class in a file named "):Code("SizeListener.quorum"):Text(". It writes the new size
into a label on the page.")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/ComboBox/SizeListener.quorum")

        Paragraph("Now give the listener to a combo box:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/ComboBox/Main2.quorum")

        Paragraph("Pick a size and notice how the label changes. Then type "):DoubleQuote():Text("Huge"):DoubleQuote():Text(" and press "):Key("Tab"):Text(".
The label says "):DoubleQuote():Text("Huge is not one of the sizes."):DoubleQuote()

        Paragraph("A program's own "):Code("Select"):Text(" doesn't tell listeners, because a person didn't
make the change. If you add "):Code("sizeBox:Select(1)"):Text(" to the program, the label still
says "):DoubleQuote():Text("No size chosen yet."):DoubleQuote()
    end

    private action WhatAScreenReaderSays
        Subsection("What a screen reader says")
        Paragraph("When a combo box gets the focus, a screen reader says its name, that it is a
combo box, and the text in the field. Then it reads the description. As the
person presses "):Key("Down"):Text(" and "):Key("Up"):Text(", the focus stays in the field, but the screen
reader is pointed at the highlighted choice, so it reads each choice as the
person moves to it. The exact words depend on the screen reader.")

        Paragraph("This is why the name matters. A combo box named "):DoubleQuote():Text("Combo Box,"):DoubleQuote():Text(" which is the
name it gets if you never set one, tells a screen reader user nothing. A
combo box named "):DoubleQuote():Text("Size"):DoubleQuote():Text(" says exactly what it asks for.")
    end

    private action Colors
        Subsection("Colors")
        Paragraph("A combo box takes its colors from the current theme, so it changes in dark
mode too. The "):Link("Themes and Dark Mode", "/tutorials/ui/themes.html"):Text(" tutorial
shows how to change them with the theme keys "):DoubleQuote():Text("ComboBox"):DoubleQuote():Text(" and "):DoubleQuote():Text("ComboBoxChoice."):DoubleQuote()
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you build a form for planning a made-up class field
trip. The form needs at least two combo boxes: one for the place to visit,
with at least eight choices, and one for the day of the week. Give each combo
box a label, a clear name, a description and a placeholder. Add a "):DoubleQuote():Text("Submit"):DoubleQuote():Text(" button. When it is pressed, output the plan if both boxes have a choice. If a
box has text that isn't one of the choices, output a message that names the
box and asks for a choice from its list.")

        Paragraph("Then decide what your form should do when someone types a place that isn't
on the list. Should it refuse it, or add it as a new choice with "):Code("Add"):Text("? Think
about two made-up users: a teacher who must approve every place, and a
student who knows a great museum that isn't listed. Write one sentence that
explains your choice and who it helps.")

        Paragraph("When it works, test it with this self-check list, using only the keyboard: "):Key("Tab"):Text(", "):Key("Down"):Text(", "):Key("Up"):Text(", "):Key("Enter"):Text(", "):Key("Escape"):Text(" and typing.")

        BulletList()
        ListItem("Can you reach both combo boxes and the button, and open, move through and
close each list?")
        ListItem("Does typing a few letters narrow the list?")
        ListItem("Does the message name the right box when you type something not on the list?")
        ListItem("Does a person who has never seen the form know what each placeholder asks
for?")
        ListItem("Turn on a screen reader (CTRL + ALT + Z on a Chromebook): does it report each combo box's
name and choice?")

        Paragraph("Fix anything that fails, then rewrite one name, description or placeholder
so it is clearer.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("A combo box is a text field with a list of choices. You can read the choice
with "):Code("GetSelectedIndex"):Text(" and "):Code("GetSelectedItem"):Text(", or react right away with a "):Code("ComboBoxListener"):Text(". Because a person can also type, a good program checks
for -1 and decides what to do with text that isn't a choice. Think back to
the warm up: which long lists on the forms you use would be easier to fill in
by typing? In the next tutorial, "):Link("Sliders", "/tutorials/ui/slider.html"):Text(", you
will let a person choose a number instead of a word.")
    end
end
